# Environment template. Copy to `.env.local` and fill in real values there.
#
# `.env.local` is git-ignored; this file is the only committed record of which
# variables exist. It contains placeholders only -- never a real server,
# database, user or password.

# The complete SQL Server / Azure SQL connection string. One variable rather
# than separate host/port/database/user/password variables, so the whole
# connection -- including encryption -- is described in one place and can differ
# per environment without a code change.
#
# SERVER ONLY. Read on the server by `lib/data/client.ts`, which is marked
# `server-only` so it can never reach a Client Component or the browser bundle.
#
# Do NOT rename this with a `NEXT_PUBLIC_` prefix: that would inline the value
# into the client bundle and publish the credentials.
#
# Keep `Encrypt=true`. Azure SQL requires TLS, and turning encryption off or
# adding `TrustServerCertificate=true` would silently downgrade transport
# security.
EVENTS_DB_CONNECTION_STRING="Server=<host>.database.windows.net,1433;Database=<database>;User Id=<user>;Password=<password>;Encrypt=true"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The origin people reach this application at, used to build every absolute
# link to an event: the host's "copy link" action, and the links in emails.
# `lib/shareLinks.ts` is the one place it is read, so a deployment changes this
# value and nothing else.
#
# An http or https origin only -- no path, query or fragment; a trailing slash
# is fine. For local development: http://localhost:3000. The deployed value is
# set in that environment.
#
# SERVER ONLY, and deliberately not taken from the request's Host header,
# which the caller controls. Do NOT add a `NEXT_PUBLIC_` prefix.
#
# When it is missing or not a bare http(s) origin, sharing is reported as
# unavailable in the host tools; the rest of the page is unaffected.
EVENTS_APP_BASE_URL="http://localhost:3000"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Email: the organization's mail service, and who the Events Board system
# sends as. Read on every send by `lib/services/emailService.ts`.
#
# LEFT COMMENTED OUT here on purpose. The real values are assigned by the
# organization's mail service and are set in the deployed environment. Unset,
# sending is refused before any request is made -- reported as "not
# configured" -- and the rest of the application is unaffected; there is no
# default sender to fall back to. Do not copy a placeholder below in as a real
# value.
#
# MAIL_SERVICE_URL -- the mail service's base URL; `/sendMail` is appended, and
#   a trailing slash is fine. Separate from EVENTS_APP_BASE_URL above, which is
#   this application's own address.
# EVENTS_MAIL_SENDER_NAME / EVENTS_MAIL_SENDER_EMAIL -- the system sender the
#   message comes from. Not the host who pressed "send": the email names them in
#   its text, but it is sent as the application.
# EVENTS_MAIL_SYSTEM_ID -- the mail service's id for this application, a whole
#   number.
#
# SERVER ONLY. Do NOT add a `NEXT_PUBLIC_` prefix to any of them.
#
# MAIL_SERVICE_URL="http://<mail-service-host>"
# EVENTS_MAIL_SENDER_NAME="<sender display name>"
# EVENTS_MAIL_SENDER_EMAIL="<sender-address>@<organization-domain>"
# EVENTS_MAIL_SYSTEM_ID="<system id assigned by the mail service>"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Authorisation for `npm run db:reset`, which DELETES every row this
# application owns: users, events, registrations, co-hosts and invites.
#
# LEAVE THIS COMMENTED OUT. It is deliberately not a setting -- and this is
# enforced, not merely requested: `npm run db:reset` reads this file and refuses
# outright if the variable is declared here, however it is set.
#
# Reset needs two independent conditions, and being able to reach the database
# is not one of them -- a working connection string is not permission to empty
# the tables. So the intent is stated at the moment of the reset, on the command
# line:
#
#     EVENTS_DB_ALLOW_RESET=yes npm run db:reset
#
# A live value in `.env.local` would pre-authorise every future reset and turn
# the guard into a one-time setup step, which is the opposite of the point.
# Node merges this file into the environment and keeps no record of where a
# variable came from, so the file itself is what reset has to inspect.
#
# Presence is not enough: the value must be exactly `yes`. Setting it to
# `false` is somebody trying to switch this off, and it is read that way.
#
# Reset also refuses outright when NODE_ENV=production, whatever this is set to.
#
# Nothing else needs this. `npm run db:check`, `npm run db:migrate` and
# `npm run db:seed` -- including `-- --status`, which reports what is in the
# tables -- ask only for the connection string above.
#
# EVENTS_DB_ALLOW_RESET=yes
